  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Global: Study on Representation, Participation, and Empowerment of Women in Customary and Informal Justice Systems</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) To understand the main structural factors and processes that advance or impede women&#x2019;s meaningful participation within CIJ systems, and how women&#x2019;s participation impacts the provision of CIJ services and access to justice &#x2013; including to formal justice &#x2013; more broadly. 2) To establish a typology of women&#x2019;s participation in CIJ systems and assess the different and varied roles that women in CIJ systems play. 3) To highlight how women&#x2019;s participation in CIJ systems constitutes or leads to the empowerment of diverse women to resolve justice problems. 4) To generate detailed recommendations for policymakers and practitioners on women&#x2019;s empowerment, gender equality and rule of law assistance. 5) To support better, more innovative, and evidence-based policy and practice that strengthens women&#x2019;s representation, participation and empowerment in CIJ systems, contributing to improved access to justice for women and in general, consonant with targets in the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development, especially those associated with Sustainable Development Goals 5 and 16.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Direct beneficiaries: Policymakers and legal practitioners; governments, especially justice and women&#x2019;s ministries and commissions; bilateral and multilateral donor agencies; United Nations agencies, especially United Nations Development Programme, Office of United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights and UN Women; international organizations; Justice Action Coalition. 
Indirect beneficiaries: Women participating in CIJ systems.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aimed to generate evidence-based recommendations for policymakers and practitioners to strengthen women&#x2019;s representation, participation and empowerment in customary and informal justice (CIJ) systems, contributing to improved access to justice for women and, more broadly, to women&#x2019;s empowerment and gender equality, the rule of law, stabilization, and peace and security.</narrative>
  </description>
